WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.600
-Our first guests tonight

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.102
are both pop culture icons

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:06.506
who've teamed up for the film
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story,"

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.207
which premieres Friday,
November 4th,

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:09.208
on the Roku Channel.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:10.409
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:14.047
[ Rock polka plays ]

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.117
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.353
-I've heard enough.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:23.089
[ Laughter ]

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.690
-And what did you think?

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:28.995
-Do you know why they call it
the music business?

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:30.729
-Uh, why?

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:31.831
-Because it's a business!

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.499
-It's a business!

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:34.934
-Use your head, kid.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.603
Nobody wants to hear
a parody song,

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:40.406
when they can hear the real
thing for the same price!

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:41.807
Wh-What's the point?

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:44.677
-Yeah, it makes no financial
sense, whatsoever.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.178
[ Laughter ]
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:49.481
our friends Daniel Radcliffe
and Weird Al Yankovic.

00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:52.518
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:52.518 --> 00:01:01.360 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.097 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:07.166
I...

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:08.901
am so happy you're both here.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.235
[ Cheering and applause
continue ]

00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:13.673
Now, I imagine, as --

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:14.774
[ Laughing ]
Please, please.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.308
Legends!

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.544
I mentioned this
to you backstage

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.346
and, hopefully, there's at least
one other person

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:21.447
who made the mistake I did.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:22.815
When I watched that scene,

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:25.485
I googled who was
the music executive

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:26.886
and it's you.
-Yes.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.788
[ Laughter ]
Like your hair is so iconic

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:29.722
that, when you have a wig on,

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:30.990
you're literally un--
-I know.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.659
Me and the Sea Captain.
-Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.494
So, gentlemen, this is based --

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.063
This is a wonderful film.

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:38.431
I laughed so hard.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.901
It's based on a short from 2010
that was on Funny or Die.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.903
-Yes.
-So explain the inspiration.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.838
-Well, Eric Appel,
who directed the movie,

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.408
also directed the original
Funny or Die clip back then

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:51.945
and I think he'd just seen
the Notorious B.I.G. biopic,

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:54.346
which plays fast and loose
with the facts.

00:01:54.346 --> 00:01:56.015
And he thought, "You know,
it would be funny

00:01:56.015 --> 00:02:00.019
to do a fake biopic trailer
for somebody who not only

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:03.789
is alive, currently,
but who, famously,

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:06.359
you know, doesn't have
a lot of scandal in their life."

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.194
And he thought of me.
Also because like

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.930
how meta would it be
to do a parody

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.498
of a guy that's famous
for parodies?

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:17.170
-Yes. And I should note that
the biggest fear

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.505
I had about this is
it's such a funny idea

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:22.341
and that the execution wouldn't
be as funny as the idea

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.977
and yet, it's so funny and there
are so many surprises in it.

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:29.148
And when did you first become
aware of Weird Al as a person?

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:31.818
-I think probably my first,
like hone--

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.119
the same way I became acquainted

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.754
with almost all American culture
before a certain age.

00:02:35.754 --> 00:02:38.157
I think the first time I heard
of you was "The Simpsons."

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:39.459
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:41.426
And, again, that's why I know
most of the things

00:02:41.426 --> 00:02:42.595
I know about America.

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:44.196
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:46.599
-We're all yellow here.
-Right, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:49.569
And then I knew a few songs
growing up

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.303
when I was a teenager,
but it was really

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:54.139
when I started dating
my girlfriend ten years ago.

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:56.942
Her and her whole family
are devoted Al fans

00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:58.578
and so that was like

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.546
the beginning
of my proper education.

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:02.681
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. You took this
very serious.

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:05.117
Not full method,
but you did grow a mustache.

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:07.186
[ Laughter ]
-Thanks. Yeah. No, no, no.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.989
There's method, and then there's
just growing some facial hair.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.191
-He made the sacrifice.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:15.161
I also -- If anyone has ever --

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.430
If anyone ever offers you
fake facial hair to wear,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.232
try not to.
Try and grow your own.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.199
-Just say no.
-Yes.

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:21.300
-I think that's a very --

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.436
I had years where I,
many times,

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.337
had to wear a fake mustache.
It's never believable

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:27.806
and it almost -- you just --
It's like talking like --

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:29.475
You feel like your teeth are
about to fall out.

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.211
-Everyone says, "Oh,
It doesn't affect how you talk,"

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.144
and then you put it on,
you're like,

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:32.978
[ Muffled ]
"I think it does

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:33.980
a little bit."

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.181
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.583
-Now, did you have a wish list,
going into this,

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:41.220
of who would play
sort of the Weird Al part?

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.522
-Daniel was number one
on the list.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.223
-Alright.
I mean, honestly, Eric and I,

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.058
we made a small list
because, you know,

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:47.527
we wanted to consider
all the options,

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.995
but we always went back
to Daniel's name

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:50.696
because -- for a number of --

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:51.898
It's like looking into a mirror

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:52.765
when I look at Daniel.
-Right, yeah.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.867
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.001
You can do a split screen
between us

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:57.703
and you wouldn't even know.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.572
No, but, you know,
Daniel's amazing

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.807
at comedic acting
and dramatic acting

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:03.809
and it's a comedy movie,
obviously,

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:06.212
but we didn't want to play it
like a comedy movie.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.780
It's meant to be like a,
you know,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.283
like a Oscar bait Hollywood
biopic, very dramatic.

00:04:11.283 --> 00:04:14.186
-It is played very straight
and that is what makes it

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:16.121
so deeply funny.
But there is --

00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:19.091
You mentioned that it plays fast
and loose with the truth,

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:21.394
which is apparent
incredibly early on,

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.796
but there are some nuggets
of truth,

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.497
which is you did get
your accordion

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:27.166
because a traveling salesman
came to the house?

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.434
-Yeah, that's the crazy thing
about this movie.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.936
Some of the things
that you would assume,

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.638
oh, we made that up,
some of the things are true.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.540
Like, you know, yeah,
I took accordion lessons

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.642
because there was, in fact,
a door-to-door salesmen

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:39.278
that came around like selling
accordion lessons.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:40.813
My first single, "My Bologna,"

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.482
was recorded
in a public bathroom.

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:43.949
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:46.619
I did singlehandedly fight
a Colombian death squad.

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:48.087
-Yes.
-No.

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.889
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:52.958
-Did you give Daniel
any accordion lessons?

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.393
Did you have to?
-Yeah.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:57.229
-Yeah. Although I have recently
come clean to Al

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:59.298
that he might've been
a tiny bit fast

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:02.067
as he was giving me lessons
and so I was maybe doing

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:03.602
a little bit of nodding along
and being like,

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.338
"Can I film you?
And I will slow this down."

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.106
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:09.008
But, yeah, but Al was
gracious enough to --

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.276
The first time we met in person,
we were sort of

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:13.479
doing recording lessons
and me sort of showing

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.147
what I had learned
up to that point.

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:17.216
Again, no one asked me
to play accordion and, in fact,

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:19.218
several times, people were like,
"It's not necessary.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.085
Please stop..."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.085 --> 00:05:23.188
But I did.
I just felt like,

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:25.892
if I was playing Al in a movie,
it just feels lazy

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:28.394
to not at least like try
to get a bit better at that.

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:31.130
-But you haven't continued on
with your learning?

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.565
-I've continued on in that
I still play

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:36.202
the four things I learned
to play on our film.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.737
And one day, you know,
maybe I'll have another lesson.

00:05:38.737 --> 00:05:40.706
-We've got an accordion
for you right here.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:41.574
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.242
-Just like that is my nightmare.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:44.176
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.111
There's a wonderful scene
at a pool

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:49.949
where there are just
a million cameos.

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:51.083
And we were talking backstage.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:52.985
Akiva Schaffer
and Jorma Taccone,

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.520
who are two-thirds
of The Lonely Island,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.290
they are in it as Alice Cooper
and Pee-Wee Herman.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:05:59.392
[ Cheering ]
I know --

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:00.359
There you go.
-Can I just

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:02.327
very quickly interrupt?
-Yeah.

00:06:02.327 --> 00:06:05.198
Jorma's Pee-Wee Herman is one
of my favorite things

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.166
about the film
and about filming it.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:10.536
Like he is so funny
and had that impression

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.271
locked and loaded,
ready to go...

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.372
-Yeah.
-...it feels like, yeah.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:14.973
-Like he's been waiting
his whole life

00:06:14.973 --> 00:06:16.909
for someone to say, "Hey,

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:20.112
are you free on a Saturday
to play Pee-Wee Herman?"

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.849
-One other thing about Jorma --
I mean, it's a low-budget movie

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:25.017
and Jorma wanted to be in it

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:28.254
and he flew himself out on his
own dime from New York

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:29.722
to be in the movie.
-Bless him.

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:31.724
-I'm sure whatever he paid him
didn't cover the plane flight.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.559
[ Laughter ]
-Right.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:34.727
Well, you can't see it onscreen.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:35.928
It looks fantastic.

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:38.698
And I should note
there are so many wonderful --

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:40.833
It seems like everybody who's
in it is having so much fun.

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.334
And because, again,

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.269
people are doing impressions
of beloved characters

00:06:44.269 --> 00:06:45.705
and they're getting
to play it straight,

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:49.008
it is such a delightful way
to approach this whole thing.

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:50.543
Also, it was just Halloween
and I have to ask

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.545
because you, I think,
are one of --

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.146
you're a costume every year.

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:57.216
You post, every year,
how many people.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:59.285
And there really is
all walks of life.

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:00.285
[ Laughter ]

00:07:00.285 --> 00:07:03.055
All ages, all sexes,

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.324
everybody is getting onboard.

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:09.428
And then, of course,
I feel like you have played --

00:07:09.428 --> 00:07:11.597
I feel like all the characters
you've played in your life

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.432
are equally represented
on Halloween.

00:07:13.432 --> 00:07:16.268
-Yes. There is a ton
of Allen Ginsbergs

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:17.470
out there every year.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.239
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:22.808
Yes. No. It's lovely.
I don't know

00:07:22.808 --> 00:07:24.377
how you feel about it
because it's actually you...

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:25.611
-No, it's lovely. It's great.
-...if that's any stranger.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.813
-It's lovely, yeah.
I really enjoy it.

00:07:27.813 --> 00:07:31.249
-You know, I imagine you,
like everybody else,

00:07:31.249 --> 00:07:33.553
have children come to your home
for Halloween.

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:37.290
Do people ever -- Do kids ever
see you and are taken aback?

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.858
-I think it's happened like once

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.260
that somebody dressed
in Potter gear

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.761
has like come to our door.

00:07:42.761 --> 00:07:46.765
It hasn't happened like a ton,
you know, other than that.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.233
But we've just moved
to a new building,

00:07:48.233 --> 00:07:50.869
so there's a whole new chance
for it to happen.

00:07:50.869 --> 00:07:53.238
But, yeah, it's mostly like
out on the street,

00:07:53.238 --> 00:07:54.873
you know, in the parade
and stuff,

00:07:54.873 --> 00:07:56.141
you see people around
and you can just be like,

00:07:56.141 --> 00:07:57.143
"Oh, yes, well done."

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:58.144
[ Laughter ]

00:07:58.144 --> 00:07:59.445
-I imagine you tried pretty hard

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:02.047
on those nights to like dress
away from the character.

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:03.615
-Yes.
If I'm leaving the house,

00:08:03.615 --> 00:08:05.451
I'm not dressing
as Harry Potter.

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:08.087
[ Laughter ]

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:10.756
-You know,
one of the nice things is,

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.691
you know,
it's referenced in the film

00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:15.961
that artists did, ultimately,
come to appreciate most

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.697
of your songs as homage.
I imagine not everyone.

00:08:18.697 --> 00:08:20.599
But is it true, because it's
a detail of the movie,

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:23.035
that it did sort of boost
the sales of the original songs?

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.203
-That is actually true.

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:26.104
They call it the "Yankovic bump"
in the movie.

00:08:26.104 --> 00:08:27.773
[ Laughter ]
But that is based on reality

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:30.509
because I heard
from Nirvana's record label

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:33.078
that, when my parody "Smells
Like Nirvana" was released,

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:36.115
they sold like an extra million
units of "Nevermind."

00:08:36.115 --> 00:08:39.251
So, you know,
artists' record sales spike

00:08:39.251 --> 00:08:41.053
when the parody comes out.
So we kind of took

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:43.355
that little nugget
and extrapolated it

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:45.123
into this whole subplot
about Madonna

00:08:45.123 --> 00:08:46.993
wanting that Yankovic bump!
You know.

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:49.328
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:51.062
-Do you have a favorite
Weird Al song?

00:08:51.062 --> 00:08:52.531
As we send people off.

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:56.135
-I mean, it changes a lot,
but at the moment,

00:08:56.135 --> 00:09:00.573
the song "Bob," speaks to the
wordy, nerdy part of my brain

00:09:00.573 --> 00:09:02.174
that is like entirely made
of palindromes.

00:09:02.174 --> 00:09:03.943
It is an amazing
Bob Dylan impression.

00:09:03.943 --> 00:09:07.045
And I also got to watch Al
perform that the other night

00:09:07.045 --> 00:09:08.313
at Carnegie Hall.
-Well, there you go.

00:09:08.313 --> 00:09:10.416
-Very, very special.
-I will say, before we go,

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:13.218
I do remember the first time
I heard "Eat It" on the radio

00:09:13.218 --> 00:09:15.020
[ Laughter ]
and, as a kid --

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.055
as a kid, I remember thinking,

00:09:17.055 --> 00:09:19.524
"This [bleep] changes
everything."

00:09:19.524 --> 00:09:21.360
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.129
And in the movie, there's the
same reaction in the movie,

00:09:24.129 --> 00:09:26.064
as a joke and, as I'm watching,
I'm like,

00:09:26.064 --> 00:09:28.200
"No, that's how I felt."

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.768
[ Laughter ]

00:09:29.768 --> 00:09:31.804
It's so great.
It is lovely to see you both.

00:09:31.804 --> 00:09:34.139
Thank you so much
for joining us tonight.

00:09:34.139 --> 00:09:36.342
[ Cheering and applause ]
Daniel Radcliffe,
Weird Al Yankovic.

